  "worked_solution": "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/bbb6555443ed4fdf7f29da4571d421b833fb21e2556a440d821bbd4e4b986923.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence II is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/6408dd510dd59615c69c063897a6ff483c20bc485d73164e74c2f57dacc4dce8.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/246210488611eed52df8d3e777e296bff30889c7dd8122d082c5d4a6bb6d7ad6.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I is an arithmetic sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
